EXT. JUNKYARD - ABANDONED SCHOOL BUS - NIGHT

A yellow bus nosed into a heap of tires, its windows long gone, its seats stuffed with sleeping bags and fairy lights run off a car battery. Beyond the fence, a refinery burns orange against the sky.

NAOMI "NOMI" ESKANDARI (17, hoodie, cigarette she doesn't smoke) sits on the bus steps flicking a lighter. INEZ BRANDÃO (16, her cousin, buzz cut, arms crossed) paces the gravel. DESHI "DAISY" OMONDI (17, the youngest-looking, glasses, a tote bag stuffed with chips) sits on an upturned bucket. And inside the bus, hidden in the dark, a fourth shape stirs under a blanket.

INEZ
Where's Wade?

NOMI
He said nine.

DAISY
It's nine-forty.

INEZ
He's not coming.

NOMI
He is. He said he'd bring the speaker.

DAISY
He said a lot of things.

INEZ
(over Daisy)
He said a lot of things last time, too.

NOMI
(over both)
Can we not?

The shape under the blanket sits up. WADE KOWALCZYK (18, bleary, a speaker the size of a brick in his lap) pulls the blanket off his head.

WADE
I've been here since seven.

NOMI
Wade.

WADE
I fell asleep.

INEZ
You fell asleep in the bus we told you not to sleep in.

WADE
It's comfortable.

DAISY
Wade, you smell like gasoline.

WADE
The tank leaks. I patched it.

NOMI
With what?

WADE
Gum.

Daisy hands him a bag of chips. He takes it without a word. Inez kicks a hubcap across the gravel. It rings.

INEZ
Next week I'm not here.

NOMI
What?

INEZ
Mom says we're moving. Tulsa.

Silence. The refinery flare hisses.

DAISY
Inez.

WADE
(overlapping)
Wait, seriously?

NOMI
(overlapping)
When did she tell you?

INEZ
Tuesday.

NOMI
It's Saturday.

INEZ
I know what day it is.

Nomi stands. She puts the lighter in her pocket. She walks to Inez and stops.

NOMI
Say it again.

INEZ
I said Tulsa.

NOMI
Not that part. The part where you didn't tell us for four days.

INEZ
Because I didn't want it to be real.

Wade turns on the speaker. A thin, tinny song starts, something old and dumb that they all know. Nobody tells him to stop. Daisy sings the first line under her breath.

Nomi lets go. She sits on the step. Inez sits beside her. Wade sets the speaker on the dashboard.

DAISY
Is there a bus in Tulsa?

INEZ
I'll find one.

WADE
I can fix it up.

INEZ
You can't even fix your tank.

WADE
It's gum. It holds.

They laugh a little. The song plays out. Over the junkyard fence, the flare finally goes dark.
